Freebuff is not a search engine and not a chat box that only writes text back. It is a loop: it reads your project, decides on one action, uses a tool, reads the result, and decides again — until the job is done or it needs something from you.

Four layers are easy to mix up:
* '''Model''' — generates the reply and chooses the next action.
* '''Agent''' — gives the model a role, instructions, your project's context and a set of tools.
* '''Tool''' — does one concrete thing: read a file, search text, run a command, open a page.
* '''Runtime''' — decides where that happens: your machine or a cloud workspace.

Freebuff is made by the Codebuff team on top of [https://codebuff.com Codebuff], an open multi-agent framework. It is often described as the free alternative to Claude Code, Codex CLI, Cursor, Windsurf and Devin.

The project is public: [https://github.com/CodebuffAI/freebuff github.com/CodebuffAI/freebuff], licence Apache-2.0.

== Who is Buffy ==
'''Buffy''' is the name of the AI agent you talk to in Freebuff — "ask Buffy" means the AI in the chat window. The same name is used for the app's mascot and on official merchandise, including a [https://freebuff.com/store/buffy-on-fire-t-shirt "Buffy on Fire" T-shirt] in the Freebuff store.

Inside Freebuff, the main working loop is called '''base3''': one agent investigates, edits and checks with its own tools. Helper agents exist on top of it — a research assistant, and in some configurations code subagents.

In Codebuff's documentation, "Buffy" is the name of the orchestrator agent. Freebuff is built on that framework, which is why the name carries over.

