== Capabilities ==
'''Finding and reading'''
* Map an unfamiliar project and explain how it fits together
* Read large files by line range instead of guessing
* Search code for a symbol, string or file name

'''Editing and running'''
* Edit files, create new ones, run tests, builds and installers in a real terminal
* Work with git: see the diff, commit, review
* Use hooks that run your own checks after a file changes

'''Checking'''
* Show a live preview of a running website next to the chat
* Drive a real browser: click, type, read the console and network errors, take screenshots

'''Shipping'''
* Publish a site with database, file storage and sign-in
* Work directly on a connected GitHub repository

'''Content'''
* Documents, slides, spreadsheets and diagrams
* Images and simple page designs

'''Information'''
* Search the web and read documentation pages
* Ask a separate research helper for multi-source answers

'''Connected apps'''
* GitHub, Linear, Notion, Stripe, Google Drive and Docs and Sheets, Discord, Telegram and others you connect
* MCP servers you add and approve

'''Parallel work'''
* Up to 6 helpers in one spawn request, and up to 20 code subagents at once with Projects access

== How to start ==
# Open [https://freebuff.com freebuff.com] and download the desktop app, or open the web app.
# Or use the terminal: <code>npm install -g freebuff</code>, then <code>freebuff</code>.
# Open a project folder, or start an empty one.
# Type a request in plain language, for example: ''"Explain what this project does."''
# Read the answer, then give the next small step.

Brief it like a ticket — three questions: '''what''' to do, '''where''' it may touch, and '''how''' you will know it worked (the command, test or URL).

Useful habits:
* Start in '''Plan''' mode on an unfamiliar project: nothing can be changed while you look around.
* One small step at a time beats "build the whole app".
* Keep the project in git so a bad change can be undone.

== Cost ==
Freebuff is funded by '''text ads''' instead of a subscription, and there are optional paid plans that give more time with models.

* Balances are counted in '''hours''' since 2026-10-15. 1 hour = 5 Freebucks.
* Each model costs hours at a rate shown in the model picker: an hour on a one-hour model uses one hour of balance, a three-hour model uses three, and free experimental models use none.
* Earned hours come from referrals and bounties, go into a wallet, and are spent after the daily allowance.

